  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1137 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nAs the trough axis translates east of the Panhandles, a brief dry \nslot and stout 120kt upper level jet streak within northwest flow \nwill dive over the region, allowing a strong cold front to push \nsouthward Monday night. This front will have a tightly packed \npressure gradient behind it, along with decently stacked jet \ndynamics. While this supports cooler temperatures being advected to \nthe Panhandles (highs in the 50s & 60s, lows in the 40s), it will\nalso usher in strong wind gusts. Additional wrap around showers \nand dense cloud cover are expected behind the front as well, \nespecially for the northern Panhandles (30-60% POPs). Even with \nthese limiting factors, it is likely that northwest winds of 20-30\nmph gusting up to 45 mph will continue through much of the day \nMonday into Tuesday. Favorable conditions behind the front could \nstill help gusts as high as 50-60 mph mix down to the surface if \neverything comes together just right (favoring the northwest \nPanhandles), so be prepared for a blustery start to the work week.\n\nLooking towards the middle of next week, models are in fair agreement \nthat a closed low will translate across the Plains in the vicinity \nof the Panhandles. This system shouldn't equate to more rain chances \nthanks to the cold front eventually purging the region of any \nmeaningful moisture. Temps should be near or below seasonal \naverages, with drier air mostly staying in place for a few days. \nMorning lows could dip into the 30s, but freezing conditions don't \nappear likely (mosquitoes across the Panhandles rejoice). Winds will \nthankfully diminish, spelling a recipe for some nice fall days. \nLooking at long range ensemble members shows that next weekend could \nbe another window of active weather opportunities, but confidence \nremains low for now.  \n\nHarrel",
